2) As rankin answered, keeping postings narrow is
the trick with google groups. To make the stuff you
write legible at all in other news readers you need
to stay under 70 column width, but Google's crowded
presentation doesn't work with even that. I post at
52 column width (and get teased for it a lot, but I
have severe astygmatism, and it's hard for me to
track a longer line, which makes proofreading hard
for me unless I use the narrow lines I do) and at 52
columns, even Google Groups presents what I post
correctly.

Also, as Thomas Mayer suggested, I use the vim
editor, which is purely wonderful, nearly as
powerful as emacs and with a much smaller memory and
disk footprint, but vim has a nearly vertical
learning curve that is Just Not Fun.  After using it
or its predecessor vi for nearly two dozen years
now, though, my fingers remember most of the
commands even when my brain is blurred by my typical
state of total exhaustion.

There's a Nutshell Handbook for vim from O'Reilly
that is very helpful, if you decide to try it, and
it also has extensive documentation integrated into
the program, accessed by the ":help" command.

